%0 Journal Article
%T Large language models can learn and generalize steganographic chain-of-thought under process supervision
%A Joey Skaf
%A Luis Ibañez-Lissen
%A Robert McCarthy
%A Connor Watts
%A Vasil Georgiv
%A Hannes Whittingham
%A L. González-Manzano
%A David Lindner
%A Cameron Tice
%A Edward James Young
%A Puria Radmard
%D 2025
%J arXiv.org
%X Chain-of-thought (CoT) reasoning not only enhances large language model performance but also provides critical insights into decision-making processes, marking it as a useful tool for monitoring model intent and planning. However, recent works have shown that banning the mention of a specific example of reward hacking causes obfuscation of the undesired reasoning traces but the persistence of the undesired behavior, threatening the reliability of CoT monitoring. We provide an extension to these results with regard to the ability of models to learn a specific type of obfuscated reasoning: steganography. First, we show that penalizing the use of specific strings within load-bearing reasoning traces causes models to substitute alternative strings. Crucially, this does not alter the underlying method by which the model performs the task, demonstrating that the model can learn to steganographically encode its reasoning.We further demonstrate that models can generalize an encoding scheme. When the penalized strings belong to an overarching class, the model learns not only to substitute strings seen in training, but also develops a general encoding scheme for all members of the class which it can apply to held-out testing strings.
%R 10.48550/arXiv.2506.01926
%U https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2506.01926
%U https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.01926
