%T A Survey on Large Reasoning Models with Self-Play Deep Reinforcement Learning and Chain-of-Thought
%A Chunfang Ou
%A Lijuan Fan
%A Guobin Fu
%A Renzheng Liu
%A Zhongzhi Li
%D 2025
%J Proceedings of the 2025 2nd Symposium on Big Data, Neural Networks, and Deep Learning
%X Large Language Models (LLMs) have achieved remarkable success in perception and generation tasks, yet their inherent limitations in complex reasoning are increasingly apparent. Chain-of-Thought (CoT) provides a viable path to enhance the reasoning capabilities of LLMs by simulating human step-by-step reasoning processes. However, standard CoT methods rely on static prompting or supervised fine-tuning, which limits the optimization and generalization of reasoning paths. Recently, integrating self-play Deep Reinforcement Learning (DRL) to optimize CoT generation has emerged as a significant research direction. This survey systematically reviews key advancements in this interdisciplinary field: Firstly, it elucidates the core concepts and evolution of large reasoning models, self-play DRL, and CoT technology. Subsequently, it provides an in-depth analysis of key technologies for CoT reasoning driven by self-play DRL, including tree search-based planning, process supervision and reward modeling, and self-play and self-improvement mechanisms. Finally, it comprehensively discusses current core challenges such as computational efficiency, reward function design, generalization, and trustworthiness, and offers prospects for future research directions, including theoretical foundations, algorithm-hardware co-design, and open-domain applications. This survey aims to provide a clear technological landscape and theoretical reference for subsequent research.
%R 10.1145/3784013.3784042
